}," are the logical (writing-mode-aware) versions. For ",[18,930,931],{},"position: absolute\u002Ffixed"," with all four set, ",[18,934,63],{},[18,936,67],{}," are derived from the insets (if not set).",[27,939,941,944],{"id":940},"z-index-and-stacking-contexts-preview",[18,942,943],{},"z-index"," and Stacking Contexts (preview)",[211,946,947],{"language":213},[215,948,950],{"className":217,"code":949,"language":213,"meta":219,"style":219},".modal { position: fixed; z-index: 1000; }\n",[18,951,952],{"__ignoreMap":219},[98,953,954,957,959,961,963,965,967,969,971,974],{"class":224,"line":225},[98,955,956],{"class":466},".modal",[98,958,232],{"class":231},[98,960,24],{"class":235},[98,962,238],{"class":231},[98,964,435],{"class":235},[98,966,597],{"class":231},[98,968,943],{"class":235},[98,970,238],{"class":231},[98,972,973],{"class":235},"1000",[98,975,847],{"class":231},[14,977,978,980,981,983,984,72,986,72,989,991,992,994],{},[18,979,943],{}," controls the stacking order of positioned elements (higher = in front). It only applies to positioned elements (not ",[18,982,392],{},"). Chapter 10 covers stacking contexts in depth — the main gotcha is that a new stacking context (from ",[18,985,693],{},[18,987,988],{},"opacity \u003C 1",[18,990,628],{},", etc.) traps children's ",[18,993,943],{}," within the parent's context.",[27,996,998],{"id":997},"normal-flow","Normal Flow",[14,1000,1001],{},"By default, block elements stack vertically; inline elements flow horizontally, wrapping as needed. Flexbox and Grid override this for their children. Understanding normal flow is essential — most layout bugs come from misunderstanding how elements flow by default.",[27,1003,1005],{"id":1004},"tips-tricks","💡 Tips & Tricks",[288,1007,1008,1028,1045,1058,1080],{},[291,1009,1010,1013,1014,64,1017,1019,1020,1022,1023,64,1025,1027],{},[104,1011,1012],{},"Idiom",": use ",[18,1015,1016],{},"display: flex",[18,1018,146],{}," for layouts (chapters 07\u002F08) — they're the modern layout tools, far more capable than floats\u002Finline-block. Reserve ",[18,1021,111],{}," for inline UI elements (buttons, badges) that need ",[18,1024,63],{},[18,1026,67],{}," but flow inline.",[291,1029,1030,1013,1032,1034,1035,1037,1038,1040,1041,1044],{},[104,1031,1012],{},[18,1033,710],{}," for sticky headers\u002Fsection headers — it's ",[18,1036,410],{}," until scrolled past, then ",[18,1039,435],{}," at the offset, all in CSS (no JavaScript scroll listeners). Ensure no ancestor has ",[18,1042,1043],{},"overflow: hidden\u002Fauto"," (which would make the sticky relative to the scroll container, not the viewport).",[291,1046,1047,1013,1049,1051,1052,1054,1055,1057],{},[104,1048,1012],{},[18,1050,454],{}," on a parent to establish a containing block for ",[18,1053,542],{}," children — the absolute child is positioned relative to the nearest positioned ancestor. Without a ",[18,1056,410],{}," parent, the absolute child is positioned relative to the viewport (or the initial containing block).",[291,1059,1060,1013,1062,1064,1065,1067,1068,1070,1071,1073,1074,1076,1077,1079],{},[104,1061,1012],{},[18,1063,330],{}," to remove from layout (no space, not accessible), ",[18,1066,334],{}," to hide but keep space, and ",[18,1069,337],{}," to hide with transitions — each has a different use. ",[18,1072,363],{}," is transitionable (good for animations); ",[18,1075,330],{}," is for genuine removal; ",[18,1078,334],{}," is for \"hidden but space reserved.\"",[291,1081,1082,1085,1086,1088,1089,64,1091,64,1093,64,1095,1097],{},[104,1083,1084],{},"Debug",": when ",[18,1087,628],{}," isn't staying fixed (it scrolls with the page), an ancestor has a ",[18,1090,693],{},[18,1092,696],{},[18,1094,699],{},[18,1096,703],{}," — that ancestor becomes the containing block instead of the viewport. Remove the transform from the ancestor, or use a different positioning strategy.",[27,1099,1101],{"id":1100},"️-edge-cases-gotchas","⚠️ Edge Cases & Gotchas",[288,1103,1104,1118,1137,1162,1188,1206,1233,1256,1265],{},[291,1105,1106,1111,1112,1114,1115,1117],{},[104,1107,1108,1110],{},[18,1109,330],{}," removes from accessibility tree and layout",": the element is gone (no space, screen readers skip it). ",[18,1113,334],{}," keeps it in the accessibility tree (screen readers may read it); ",[18,1116,337],{}," keeps it interactive.",[291,1119,1120,238,1128,1131,1132,1134,1135,324],{},[104,1121,1122,1123,64,1125,1127],{},"Inline elements ignore ",[18,1124,63],{},[18,1126,67],{},"\u002Fvertical margin",[18,1129,1130],{},"span { width: 100px; }"," does nothing. Use ",[18,1133,111],{}," or ",[18,1136,57],{},[291,1138,1139,1149,1150,64,1152,64,1154,64,1156,1158,1159,1161],{},[104,1140,1141,1143,1144,1148],{},[18,1142,542],{}," is relative to the nearest ",[1145,1146,1147],"em",{},"positioned"," ancestor",": if no ancestor is ",[18,1151,410],{},[18,1153,425],{},[18,1155,435],{},[18,1157,445],{},", it's relative to the viewport (the initial containing block). Forgetting ",[18,1160,454],{}," on the parent is a common bug.",[291,1163,1164,1172,1173,64,1175,64,1177,64,1179,1181,1182,1184,1185,1187],{},[104,1165,1166,1168,1169,1171],{},[18,1167,628],{}," and ",[18,1170,693],{}," ancestors",": an ancestor with ",[18,1174,693],{},[18,1176,696],{},[18,1178,699],{},[18,1180,703],{}," becomes the containing block for ",[18,1183,435],{},", so the \"fixed\" element scrolls with that ancestor. Surprise when you add a ",[18,1186,693],{}," for an animation and the fixed header starts scrolling.",[291,1189,1190,1198,1199,64,1201,64,1203,1205],{},[104,1191,1192,1194,1195,1171],{},[18,1193,710],{}," needs a tall enough parent and no ",[18,1196,1197],{},"overflow",": if the parent is the same height as the sticky element, it can't stick (no room to scroll). An ancestor with ",[18,1200,771],{},[18,1202,774],{},[18,1204,777],{}," makes the sticky relative to that scroll container.",[291,1207,1208,238,1213,1216,1217,1219,1220,1222,1223,64,1225,64,1227,1229,1230,1232],{},[104,1209,1210,1212],{},[18,1211,943],{}," only works on positioned elements",[18,1214,1215],{},"z-index: 10"," on a ",[18,1218,392],{}," element does nothing. Set ",[18,1221,454],{}," (or ",[18,1224,425],{},[18,1226,435],{},[18,1228,445],{},") for ",[18,1231,943],{}," to apply.",[291,1234,1235,1240,1241,1243,1244,1247,1248,1251,1252,1255],{},[104,1236,1237,1238],{},"Stacking contexts trap ",[18,1239,943],{},": a child's ",[18,1242,943],{}," is relative to its parent's stacking context. A ",[18,1245,1246],{},"z-index: 9999"," inside a ",[18,1249,1250],{},"z-index: 1"," parent can't appear above a ",[18,1253,1254],{},"z-index: 2"," sibling of the parent. See chapter 10.",[291,1257,1258,1264],{},[104,1259,1260,1263],{},[18,1261,1262],{},"display: contents"," removes the box but keeps children",": the element's box disappears (no margin\u002Fpadding\u002Fborder), and children behave as if they're children of the grandparent. Useful for semantic wrappers that shouldn't affect layout. Some accessibility issues (older browsers).",[291,1266,1267,238,1272,1275,1276,1279],{},[104,1268,1269,1271],{},[18,1270,793],{}," shorthand (2021+)",[18,1273,1274],{},"inset: 0"," is shorthand for ",[18,1277,1278],{},"top: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0; left: 0;"," — useful for full-coverage absolute positioning. Older browsers need the longhand.",[27,1281,1283],{"id":1282},"spot-the-bug","🧠 Spot the Bug",[14,1285,1286,1287,1290],{},"A developer adds a ",[18,1288,1289],{},"transform: scale(1.05)"," to a header for a hover effect, and suddenly the fixed navigation bar below it starts scrolling with the page:",[211,1292,1293],{"language":213},[215,1294,1296],{"className":217,"code":1295,"language":213,"meta":219,"style":219},".page { transform: scale(1); }   \u002F* applied to a wrapper around everything *\u002F\n.navbar { position: fixed; top: 0; }\n",[18,1297,1298,1324],{"__ignoreMap":219},[98,1299,1300,1303,1305,1307,1309,1312,1315,1318,1321],{"class":224,"line":225},[98,1301,1302],{"class":466},".page",[98,1304,232],{"class":231},[98,1306,693],{"class":235},[98,1308,238],{"class":231},[98,1310,1311],{"class":235},"scale",[98,1313,1314],{"class":231},"(",[98,1316,1317],{"class":235},"1",[98,1319,1320],{"class":231},"); }   ",[98,1322,1323],{"class":246},"\u002F* applied to a wrapper around everything *\u002F\n",[98,1325,1326,1328,1330,1332,1334,1336,1338,1340,1342,1344],{"class":224,"line":250},[98,1327,640],{"class":466},[98,1329,232],{"class":231},[98,1331,24],{"class":235},[98,1333,238],{"class":231},[98,1335,435],{"class":235},[98,1337,597],{"class":231},[98,1339,399],{"class":235},[98,1341,238],{"class":231},[98,1343,594],{"class":235},[98,1345,847],{"class":231},[14,1347,1348],{},"What's happening?",[1350,1351,1352,1356,1381,1387,1490,1512],"details",{},[1353,1354,1355],"summary",{},"Answer",[14,1357,1358,1359,1361,1362,1364,1365,1367,1368,1371,1372,1374,1375,1377,1378,1380],{},"An ancestor (",[18,1360,1302],{},", which wraps everything including ",[18,1363,640],{},") has a ",[18,1366,693],{},", which makes it the ",[104,1369,1370],{},"containing block"," for ",[18,1373,628],{}," descendants. So ",[18,1376,640],{}," is no longer fixed relative to the viewport — it's fixed relative to ",[18,1379,1302],{},", which scrolls with the content. The \"fixed\" navbar scrolls.",[14,1382,1383,1384,1386],{},"The fix — remove the ",[18,1385,693],{}," from the ancestor that wraps the fixed element, or move the fixed element outside the transformed ancestor:",[215,1388,1390],{"className":217,"code":1389,"language":213,"meta":219,"style":219},"\u002F* Option 1: don't transform the wrapper *\u002F\n.page { \u002F* no transform *\u002F }\n\n\u002F* Option 2: move the navbar outside .page in the HTML *\u002F\n\u003Cbody>\n    \u003Cnav class=\"navbar\">...\u003C\u002Fnav>   \u003C!-- outside .page -->\n    \u003Cdiv class=\"page\">...\u003C\u002Fdiv>\n\u003C\u002Fbody>\n",[18,1391,1392,1397,1409,1413,1418,1429,1462,1480],{"__ignoreMap":219},[98,1393,1394],{"class":224,"line":225},[98,1395,1